The code is almost correct, but may make one unnecessary request after the last page. Add an explicit check for `batch` being empty or missing to robustly terminate the loop.

The timer is never removed, so `timers` grows indefinitely for requests that complete, causing a memory leak. Reusing a `requestId` also overwrites the previous request’s start time, making its elapsed value incorrect.

Use a cleanup operation in a `finally` block, and ensure IDs are unique:

```js
const timers = new Map();

function startTimer(requestId) {
  timers.set(requestId, performance.now());
}

function elapsed(requestId) {
  const start = timers.get(requestId);
  return start === undefined ? null : performance.now() - start;
}

function stopTimer(requestId) {
  const duration = elapsed(requestId);
  timers.delete(requestId);
  return duration;
}
```

Call `stopTimer(requestId)` when the request finishes, including error paths. `performance.now()` avoids incorrect durations when the system clock changes.

